---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const steps = [
  { n: "01", t: "Shade",    s: ["Covered for the last", "20 to 40 days"] },
  { n: "02", t: "Pick",     s: ["Youngest leaves,", "first harvest only"] },
  { n: "03", t: "Steam",    s: ["Within hours,", "to stop oxidation"] },
  { n: "04", t: "Dry",      s: ["Cooled, then", "dried flat"] },
  { n: "05", t: "Destem",   s: ["Stem and vein out.", "This is tencha"] },
  { n: "06", t: "Grind",    s: ["Granite, slowly.", "30 to 40g an hour"] },
];

const essays = [
  {
    title: "My Lifelong Career as a Reader and Writer",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 6, page 26",
    href: "/writing/reader-and-writer/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2026/03/Translingual-Issue-6.pdf",
    note: "How I came to reading through my brother&rsquo;s birthday books, and what writing turned into after that.",
  },
  {
    title: "A Film Analysis: Grave of the Fireflies",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, page 16",
    href: "/writing/grave-of-the-fireflies/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2023/04/Translingual-V1.-I2.-Final.pdf",
    note: "How the film does what it does to you without ever frightening you.",
  },
  {
    title: "Shades of Black",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, page 42",
    href: "/writing/shades-of-black/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2023/04/Translingual-V1.-I2.-Final.pdf",
    note: "Why I wear black, and what I found underneath the habit after my grandfather died.",
  },
  {
    title: "Imprisonment in The Allegory of the Cave and The House of Asterion",
    where: "Unpublished",
    href: "/writing/imprisonment/",
    note: "Plato&rsquo;s cave and Borges&rsquo;s minotaur, read against each other.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Rough to Cut"
  description="The inspirations behind this site. Matcha as a morning ritual, semi-precious stones, and four essays. By Linh (Min) Le."
>
  <article class="rough">

    <header class="band g-cream rough-head">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Beyond work</p>
        <h1>Rough to Cut</h1>
        <p class="rough-lede">
          Curious about inspirations behind this site? Green and soft cream palettes running through
          every page. Fragments that form into a polished stone at first landing. And
          longer-than-necessary narratives under each work. They are intentional, yes, guided by
          instinct and shaped by fragments of what interests me outside of work.
        </p>
        <p class="rough-sub">
          Nothing professional below the line. Each piece, still, is a piece of my identity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band plated g-beige" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell rough-split">
        <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Matcha</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>I like how particular it is</h2>

        <p>
          I make matcha every morning. There is a bowl and a whisk at home and a corner of the
          kitchen that is only for this, and the whole thing takes about twenty minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I like is how particular it is. The water has to be off the boil rather than at it.
          The powder gets sifted or it clumps. The whisk moves in a quick W until a fine foam comes
          up across the surface, and you can tell straight away if you rushed. It is a small set of
          steps that reward being done properly, and doing them properly is what settles me. I have
          never got the same thing out of anything loose.
        </p>
        <p>
          How matcha is made interests me for the same reason. The plants are shaded for the last
          twenty to forty days before harvest, and the lack of light pushes them to make more
          chlorophyll and turn their catechins into L-theanine, which is where the colour and the
          sweetness come from. Only the youngest leaves are picked. They are steamed within hours so
          they cannot oxidise, then dried, then stripped of stem and vein until only the leaf is
          left. That part is called tencha. It becomes matcha when it goes between two granite
          stones, slowly, about thirty grams an hour, because grinding faster heats the powder and
          takes the smell out of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I find it reassuring that so much of it is deliberate and none of it can be hurried.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also does something coffee has never done for me. The L-theanine comes along with the
          caffeine, so I get clear and stay clear for a few hours instead of spiking and dropping.
          Coffee gives me an hour and takes it back in the afternoon.
        </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img src="/media/rough/matcha-whisk.jpg" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="A bamboo chasen lifted out of a white ceramic bowl, matcha whisked to a flat green surface." />
          <figcaption>The whisk is bamboo, cut into a hundred tines, and it is the only tool in the process I own.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-leaf" style="--plate: var(--g-ivory);">
      <div class="shell rough-split mirror">
        <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Stones</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>I collect the cheap ones on purpose</h2>

        <p>
          One of the first places I found after moving to Cincinnati was GeoRarities, a rock shop in
          a suite inside an art studio building. You would walk past it. I go back a few times a
          year, and most of what I own came from small shops like that one.
        </p>
        <p>
          I collect semi-precious stones. The name means they were ranked below the precious ones,
          and I prefer them for it. An expensive stone arrives with its value already settled and
          leaves you nothing to think about. A cheap one you actually have to look at. Whatever it
          is worth comes from where it formed, how long that took, and what people have made of it
          over a few thousand years.
        </p>
        <p>
          My element in Eastern philosophy is Water, so what I pick up is nearly always blue or
          white.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lapis is my favourite. It is a rock rather than one mineral: lazurite makes the blue,
          calcite runs through it in thin white lines, and pyrite shows up as small gold flecks. The
          Egyptians used it in burial masks and ground it down for a blue that painters could barely
          afford. People mix it up with sodalite, which looks similar indoors, though sodalite has
          almost no pyrite in it, so in daylight lapis catches the light in a way sodalite never
          does.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I like most is what it is supposed to mean. It gets called the stone of wisdom, and
          most descriptions leave that at clear thinking. The ones I like go further, to being
          believed when you say the clear thing. That has always been the harder part for me, and I
          like carrying something people have associated with it since the pharaohs.
        </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img src="/media/rough/lapis-lazuli.jpg" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Tumbled lapis lazuli stones, deep blue with white calcite veining and small flecks of gold pyrite." />
          <figcaption>Blue lazurite, white calcite, and the gold pyrite that tells it apart from sodalite.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-beige" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Writing</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>I was writing long before I was analysing anything</h2>
        <p>
          Three of these were printed in Translingual, which Ohio State&rsquo;s International English
          Language Programs publishes. The one about Plato and Borges I wrote for the same class and
          never sent anywhere. You can read all four here.
        </p>

        <ol class="essays">
          {essays.map((e) => (
            <li class="essay">
              <h3 class="essay-title"><a href={e.href}>{e.title}</a></h3>
              <p class="essay-where figure">
                {e.where}
                {e.published && (
                  <> &middot; <a href={e.published} target="_blank" rel="noopener">as published</a></>
                )}
              </p>
              <p class="essay-note">{e.note}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band g-leaf rough-end">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p>
          That is what the hours outside work go to. If you came in from a case study, the
          rest of the site is one click back.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-out">
          <a class="cta ghost" href="/#work">Back to the work</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .rough-head { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .rough-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -.02em;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .rough-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin-top: .9rem;
    padding-top: .9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }
  .rough-lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .rough-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 23rem);
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 3.25rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .rough-split.mirror { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 23rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .rough-split.mirror .shot { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }
  .rough-split.mirror .prose { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; }
  .rough-split .shot { position: sticky; top: 5.5rem; margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .rough-split, .rough-split.mirror { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .rough-split.mirror .shot, .rough-split .shot { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; position: static; }
    .rough-split.mirror .prose { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; }
    .rough-split .shot { max-width: 34rem; margin-top: 1.6rem; }
  }

  .prose { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -.015em;
    margin: .3rem 0 1.3rem;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1.05em; line-height: 1.62; }
  .rough-end { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .rough-end .prose p { max-width: 52ch; }
  .prose .beat {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 60, "WONK" 1;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .shot { margin: 1.8rem 0 2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .7rem; }
  .shot img {
    width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .shot figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    padding-left: .75rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-line);
  }

  .fig { margin: 1.6rem 0 1.8rem; }
  .fig-frame {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: .8rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .fig-frame svg { display: block; width: 100%; min-width: 34rem; height: auto; }
  .st rect { fill: none; stroke: var(--rule-strong); stroke-width: 1; }
  .st.on rect { fill: var(--accent-wash); stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .sn  { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .1em; }
  .stt { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; fill: var(--ink); }
  .st.on .stt { fill: var(--accent-deep); }
  .sss { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 7.5px; fill: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cap { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .arw { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.2; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; }

  .essays { list-style: none; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .essay { padding: 1.1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .essay:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }
  .essay-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 40;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .essay-title a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent-line); }
  .essay-title a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); border-color: var(--accent); }
  .essay-where {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .05em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .essay-note { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-top: .45rem; line-height: 1.55; }

  .closing-out { margin-top: .8rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; text-decoration: none;
    background: var(--accent-deep); color: var(--g-ivory);
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-deep); border-radius: 7px;
    padding: .6rem .95rem; display: inline-block;
    transition: background .25s ease, border-color .25s ease, color .25s ease;
  }
  .cta.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--ink); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 34%, transparent); }
  .cta.ghost:hover { background: var(--accent-wash); border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-deep); }
  .cta:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
</style>
